robertwh:{"n":"Robert Whatmough","d":"c. 1815–1887 · Rochdale","b":1815,"x":1887,"s":"p","p":["benjaminw","sarahbr"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"A Lancashire man from Rochdale, where his brother James was born, he was one of the early fruit growers of the Plenty River country north-east of Melbourne. By 1853 he was showing fruit at the Melbourne horticultural shows, taking a second prize for a general collection, and in February 1856 both the Age and the Argus singled out his apples, grown on the River Plenty and on show at Law and Gordon's in Melbourne, as proof of what the colony could grow: White Moss and Kentish Fill-basket apples averaging sixteen inches round.\n\nHe was a proud and plain-spoken grower. In March 1860, after showing twenty-five varieties of dessert, cooking and cider apples at the Victoria Agricultural Society, he wrote to the Age to complain that he should have had first prize, and that two of the judges were unfit for the job, having mistaken three of his russet apples for pears. The society later thanked him for accepting a certificate of merit instead of his prize money. In 1870 he showed four apples at the Horticultural Society, one a seedling of his own that the committee named the Greensborough Pippin, and a seedling plum named Whatmough's Prolific. A peach he raised, Whatmough's Seedling, was still on the recommended lists of peaches for Victoria in the 1880s.\n\nHe settled at Diamond Creek, beside Greensborough, where his daughter Sarah married Joseph Wilson in 1866, and he died there in 1887, aged 72.","src":[["Victorian BDM history search","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Apples from the Plenty, Age, 18 Feb 1856","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/154863675"],["Fruit from the Plenty district, Argus, 29 Feb 1856","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/4831954"],["Fair play: letter from Robert Whatmough, Age, 26 Mar 1860","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/154880205"],["Horticultural Society prizes, Argus, 5 Mar 1860","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/5678260"],["Greensborough Pippin and Whatmough's Prolific, Age, 3 Feb 1870","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/189326776"],["Whatmough's Seedling peach, Leader, 12 Jun 1880","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/196694398"]]},
